export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","addon":"cabinet","name":"Cabinets","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"cabinet-start","scope":"addon:cabinet","title":"Cabinets: getting started","summary":"What the Cabinets add-on does in CadCraft, how to switch it on, and how to place a run of cabinets along a wall.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["cabinet","cabinets","kitchen","millwork","casework","joinery","base cabinet","wall cabinet","tall cabinet","drawers","frameless","32 mm","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-run-of-cabinets","text":"Your first run of cabinets","level":2},{"id":"units","text":"Units","level":2},{"id":"files-open-anywhere","text":"Files open anywhere","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECabinets is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. You place cabinets along a wall in the plan, and CadCraft works out every panel of every cabinet from your construction rules. From the drawing it makes what the shop needs: a cut list by material, how much edge banding the job takes, a nest of the panels on sheets, the nested sheets as a DXF for your CAM program, and a label with a bar code for every piece in the order the pieces come off the machine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinets\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinets\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinets\u003C/span\u003E, as a group at the end of the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-run-of-cabinets\"\u003EYour first run of cabinets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECABINET\u003C/code\u003E. Click the two ends of the wall line (snap to the wall&#039;s face), then click anywhere on the room side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the width of the first base cabinet (\u003Ccode\u003E600\u003C/code\u003E) and press Enter, or click where it ends. The next one starts where it stops: type \u003Ccode\u003E800\u003C/code\u003E for the next.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E to leave a gap (for a range or a dishwasher), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EW\u003C/span\u003E for wall cabinets (they start again at the wall&#039;s start, hung above), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ED\u003C/span\u003E for a drawer base, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E for a tall cabinet, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EU\u003C/span\u003E to take the last one back. Press Enter when the run is done.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECABELEV\u003C/code\u003E, click one of the cabinets and click where the front view goes. It shows every cabinet on that wall with its doors and drawers, and it follows when you move or change a cabinet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a cabinet to change its size, kind, shelves, doors or drawers in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole run is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"units\"\u003EUnits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing in inches, feet or yards uses inch rules (3/4 in carcass, 1/8 in gaps); any other drawing uses millimetre rules (18 mm carcass, 3 mm gaps). In a drawing in metres, type \u003Ccode\u003E0.6\u003C/code\u003E for a 600 mm cabinet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-open-anywhere\"\u003EFiles open anywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA cabinet and a front view are each saved as a block reference with its drawing inside, and its data kept with it under the application name \u003Ccode\u003ECC_CABINET\u003C/code\u003E. A drawing saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program as drawn, and in CadCraft without the add-on with nothing lost.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Cabinets is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. You place cabinets along a wall in the plan, and CadCraft works out every panel of every cabinet from your construction rules. From the drawing it makes what the shop needs: a cut list by material, how much edge banding the job takes, a nest of the panels on sheets, the nested sheets as a DXF for your CAM program, and a label with a bar code for every piece in the order the pieces come off the machine. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › Cabinets. Its tools are in the Cabinets section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in Settings › CadCraft › Cabinets, as a group at the end of the toolbar. Your first run of cabinets Type CABINET. Click the two ends of the wall line (snap to the wall's face), then click anywhere on the room side. Type the width of the first base cabinet (600) and press Enter, or click where it ends. The next one starts where it stops: type 800 for the next. Type G to leave a gap (for a range or a dishwasher), W for wall cabinets (they start again at the wall's start, hung above), D for a drawer base, T for a tall cabinet, U to take the last one back. Press Enter when the run is done. Type CABELEV, click one of the cabinets and click where the front view goes. It shows every cabinet on that wall with its doors and drawers, and it follows when you move or change a cabinet. Select a cabinet to change its size, kind, shelves, doors or drawers in Properties. The whole run is one undo step. Units A drawing in inches, feet or yards uses inch rules (3/4 in carcass, 1/8 in gaps); any other drawing uses millimetre rules (18 mm carcass, 3 mm gaps). In a drawing in metres, type 0.6 for a 600 mm cabinet. Files open anywhere A cabinet and a front view are each saved as a block reference with its drawing inside, and its data kept with it under the application name CC_CABINET. A drawing saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program as drawn, and in CadCraft without the add-on with nothing lost."},{"slug":"cabinet-cutlist","scope":"addon:cabinet","title":"Cabinets: cut list, nesting and labels","summary":"The cut list by material, edge-banding totals, nesting the panels on sheets, the DXF for your CAM program, and a bar-coded label for every piece.","section":"tasks","order":2,"keywords":["cut list","cutting list","edge banding","banding","nesting","nest","sheets","cnc","router","cam","dxf","labels","barcode","bar code","code 128","csv"],"headings":[{"id":"the-cut-list","text":"The cut list","level":2},{"id":"nesting","text":"Nesting","level":2},{"id":"labels","text":"Labels","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-cut-list\"\u003EThe cut list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECABLIST\u003C/code\u003E. CadCraft lists how many pieces each material takes, the area to cut and about how many sheets that is, and how many metres (or feet) of each edge banding the job needs. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinet cut list (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E saves every part as a row for a spreadsheet: its id (\u003Ccode\u003EB01-03\u003C/code\u003E = cabinet B01, part 3), name, cabinet, material, thickness, finished size, quantity, grain, the banding on each edge (L1 and L2 run along the length, W1 and W2 along the width; L1 is the front edge) and the cut size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nesting\"\u003ENesting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECABNEST\u003C/code\u003E. Each material is nested on its own sheets (2440 × 1220 mm or 96 × 48 in, set in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinets\u003C/span\u003E with the edge kept clear and the space between parts: your cutter&#039;s diameter and a little more). Parts with grain stay along the sheet&#039;s length. CadCraft says how many sheets each material takes and how much of them is used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinet sheets, nested (DXF)\u003C/span\u003E saves every sheet in one DXF: the sheet outlines on layer \u003Ccode\u003ESHEET\u003C/code\u003E, the parts on \u003Ccode\u003EPARTS\u003C/code\u003E, each named by its piece id and its number on the sheet on \u003Ccode\u003ELABELS\u003C/code\u003E. Open it in your CAM program (PartCraft&#039;s Manufacture, or your router&#039;s own software) to make the toolpaths and the G-code.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"labels\"\u003ELabels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinet labels (HTML, print at 100 %)\u003C/span\u003E saves a page for every label sheet. Print it at 100 % (no fit to page) on the label sheet chosen in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinets\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabel sheet\u003C/span\u003E: A4 or Letter sheets, or one label a page for a label printer. Each label has the part&#039;s name and cabinet, its size and material, its sheet and number in the nest, a small drawing of the part with its banded edges drawn heavy and the grain as an arrow, and a Code 128 bar code of its piece id.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELabels in\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECutting order (nest)\u003C/span\u003E the labels come in the order the pieces come off the sheets, so you can label each piece as it is cut.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The cut list Type CABLIST. CadCraft lists how many pieces each material takes, the area to cut and about how many sheets that is, and how many metres (or feet) of each edge banding the job needs. EXPORT › Cabinet cut list (CSV) saves every part as a row for a spreadsheet: its id (B01-03 = cabinet B01, part 3), name, cabinet, material, thickness, finished size, quantity, grain, the banding on each edge (L1 and L2 run along the length, W1 and W2 along the width; L1 is the front edge) and the cut size. Nesting Type CABNEST. Each material is nested on its own sheets (2440 × 1220 mm or 96 × 48 in, set in Settings › CadCraft › Cabinets with the edge kept clear and the space between parts: your cutter's diameter and a little more). Parts with grain stay along the sheet's length. CadCraft says how many sheets each material takes and how much of them is used. EXPORT › Cabinet sheets, nested (DXF) saves every sheet in one DXF: the sheet outlines on layer SHEET, the parts on PARTS, each named by its piece id and its number on the sheet on LABELS. Open it in your CAM program (PartCraft's Manufacture, or your router's own software) to make the toolpaths and the G-code. Labels EXPORT › Cabinet labels (HTML, print at 100 %) saves a page for every label sheet. Print it at 100 % (no fit to page) on the label sheet chosen in Settings › CadCraft › Cabinets › Label sheet: A4 or Letter sheets, or one label a page for a label printer. Each label has the part's name and cabinet, its size and material, its sheet and number in the nest, a small drawing of the part with its banded edges drawn heavy and the grain as an arrow, and a Code 128 bar code of its piece id. With Labels in › Cutting order (nest) the labels come in the order the pieces come off the sheets, so you can label each piece as it is cut."},{"slug":"cabinet-hardware","scope":"addon:cabinet","title":"Cabinets: drilling, hardware, side view and 3D","summary":"The 32 mm drilling of every cabinet, the hardware list, a side view cut through a cabinet, and the cabinets on the 3D tab.","section":"tasks","order":3,"keywords":["drilling","32 mm","system holes","shelf pins","hinge","hinge cup","plate","drawer slide","hardware","legs","side view","section","3d","solids"],"headings":[{"id":"drilling","text":"Drilling","level":2},{"id":"hardware-list","text":"Hardware list","level":2},{"id":"side-view","text":"Side view","level":2},{"id":"3d","text":"3D","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"drilling\"\u003EDrilling\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECABDRILL\u003C/code\u003E. CadCraft lists the holes of every cabinet part that is drilled. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinet drilling (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E writes every hole as a row: the part, its face, the kind of hole, \u003Cstrong\u003Ex\u003C/strong\u003E along the part&#039;s length, \u003Cstrong\u003Ey\u003C/strong\u003E across its width, the diameter and the depth.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESides\u003C/strong\u003E are drilled on the inside face. \u003Cstrong\u003Ey\u003C/strong\u003E runs from the back edge to the front edge; \u003Cstrong\u003Ex\u003C/strong\u003E from the floor (a notched kick) or from the bottom of the box. Shelf-pin holes sit on two lines, 37 mm from the front and the back edge, 32 mm apart, the first 64 mm from the end. Hinge plates take two of the same holes on the hinge side only. Drawer slides take a front and a rear hole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDoors\u003C/strong\u003E are drilled on the back face for cup hinges (Ø35, 12.5 deep, the centre 22.5 mm from the hinge edge, 100 mm from each end; two hinges up to 1000 mm, three to 1600, four to 2200). \u003Cstrong\u003Ey\u003C/strong\u003E counts from the \u003Cstrong\u003Ehinge edge\u003C/strong\u003E. A pair of doors is one part of two: the second is the mirror image.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA side is one part of quantity two, drilled on its inside face. Two sides are mirror images of each other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery number (the 37, 32 and 64, the cup size, the hinge spacing) is one of the construction rules, so a shop with other numbers changes them. The numbers are the common frameless-cabinet ones; check them against your hardware maker&#039;s sheet before you drill.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hardware-list\"\u003EHardware list\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECABHARD\u003C/code\u003E. It counts hinges and plates, shelf pins (four a shelf), drawer slides by length, legs (four a base cabinet, six over 900 mm wide), hanging brackets, pulls (one a front) and panel fasteners (two a joint). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinet hardware list (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E saves it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"side-view\"\u003ESide view\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECABSIDE\u003C/code\u003E: pick a cabinet and a point. The cabinet is drawn cut through its middle and seen from the side: the side panel, bottom, top or rails, back, shelves, plinth, the fronts and each drawer box. It follows the cabinet when its size or rules change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d\"\u003E3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECAB3D\u003C/code\u003E puts the cabinets on the 3D tab as solids (panels, shelves, plinth, fronts). With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinets\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep the cabinets on the 3D tab\u003C/span\u003E on, they follow every change you make.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Drilling Type CABDRILL. CadCraft lists the holes of every cabinet part that is drilled. EXPORT › Cabinet drilling (CSV) writes every hole as a row: the part, its face, the kind of hole, x along the part's length, y across its width, the diameter and the depth. Sides are drilled on the inside face. y runs from the back edge to the front edge; x from the floor (a notched kick) or from the bottom of the box. Shelf-pin holes sit on two lines, 37 mm from the front and the back edge, 32 mm apart, the first 64 mm from the end. Hinge plates take two of the same holes on the hinge side only. Drawer slides take a front and a rear hole. Doors are drilled on the back face for cup hinges (Ø35, 12.5 deep, the centre 22.5 mm from the hinge edge, 100 mm from each end; two hinges up to 1000 mm, three to 1600, four to 2200). y counts from the hinge edge. A pair of doors is one part of two: the second is the mirror image. A side is one part of quantity two, drilled on its inside face. Two sides are mirror images of each other. Every number (the 37, 32 and 64, the cup size, the hinge spacing) is one of the construction rules, so a shop with other numbers changes them. The numbers are the common frameless-cabinet ones; check them against your hardware maker's sheet before you drill. Hardware list Type CABHARD. It counts hinges and plates, shelf pins (four a shelf), drawer slides by length, legs (four a base cabinet, six over 900 mm wide), hanging brackets, pulls (one a front) and panel fasteners (two a joint). EXPORT › Cabinet hardware list (CSV) saves it. Side view CABSIDE: pick a cabinet and a point. The cabinet is drawn cut through its middle and seen from the side: the side panel, bottom, top or rails, back, shelves, plinth, the fronts and each drawer box. It follows the cabinet when its size or rules change. 3D CAB3D puts the cabinets on the 3D tab as solids (panels, shelves, plinth, fronts). With Settings › Cabinets › Keep the cabinets on the 3D tab on, they follow every change you make."},{"slug":"cabinet-construction","scope":"addon:cabinet","title":"Cabinets: how they are built","summary":"The construction rules the Cabinets add-on builds with, how each part's size is worked out, and how to change the rules.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["construction","frameless","32 mm system","carcass","back","groove","toe kick","plinth","legs","reveal","door","drawer","slide","shelf","rules"],"headings":[{"id":"the-default-frameless-32-mm-system","text":"The default: frameless, 32 mm system","level":2},{"id":"doors-and-drawers","text":"Doors and drawers","level":2},{"id":"changing-the-rules","text":"Changing the rules","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-default-frameless-32-mm-system\"\u003EThe default: frameless, 32 mm system\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery cabinet is a box of panels: the sides run the full height of the box, the bottom (and on wall and tall cabinets the top) sits between the sides, and a base cabinet has two rails across the top instead of a top panel, so the worktop can be fixed through them. The back sits in grooves in the sides and the bottom. Base and tall cabinets stand on adjustable legs with a plinth board in front. Doors and drawer fronts cover the box (full overlay) with an even gap all round.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat a 600 mm base cabinet (870 high, 560 deep) is made of with the millimetre rules:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPart\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESize (mm)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEdge banding\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESide × 2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E720 × 560 × 18\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efront edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBottom\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E564 × 560 × 18\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efront edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFront rail\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E564 × 100 × 18\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efront edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBack rail\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E564 × 100 × 18\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBack\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E580 × 710 × 8\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShelf\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E562 × 516 × 18\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efront edge\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDoor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E717 × 597 × 18\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eall four edges\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlinth\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E600 × 150 × 18\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enone\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe box is 870 − 150 = 720 high. Inside, it is 600 − 2 × 18 = 564 wide. The back goes 8 mm into each groove. The shelf is 1 mm short at each end and stops 20 mm behind the front. The door is 3 mm smaller than the box each way, so two doors side by side have a 3 mm gap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESizes are \u003Cstrong\u003Efinished sizes\u003C/strong\u003E, with the banding on. The cut list also gives the cut size: the banding&#039;s thickness comes off each banded edge.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"doors-and-drawers\"\u003EDoors and drawers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA cabinet wider than one door may be (600 mm or 24 in) gets a pair. A tall cabinet whose door would be taller than 1400 mm (55 in) gets two rows of doors with a fixed shelf between them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDoors\u003C/span\u003E sets one, two or none.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA drawer base has fronts of equal height (or the heights you give). Each drawer box is as wide as the opening less the slide gap at each side (13 mm), as deep as the longest slide that fits, and 40 mm lower than its front. Its bottom sits in 6 mm grooves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"changing-the-rules\"\u003EChanging the rules\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECabinets\u003C/span\u003E sets the carcass thickness, the gap between fronts, the back (in grooves, between the panels, or on the back), the back&#039;s thickness and the toe kick (legs and a plinth, notched sides, or none). The settings apply to cabinets you place from then on: each cabinet keeps the rules it was built with, so a drawing gives the same cut list wherever it is opened.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA drafting aid.\u003C/strong\u003E The rules are common shop practice, not any maker&#039;s system. Check the sizes against the hardware you will fit (hinges, slides, legs) before you cut.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The default: frameless, 32 mm system Every cabinet is a box of panels: the sides run the full height of the box, the bottom (and on wall and tall cabinets the top) sits between the sides, and a base cabinet has two rails across the top instead of a top panel, so the worktop can be fixed through them. The back sits in grooves in the sides and the bottom. Base and tall cabinets stand on adjustable legs with a plinth board in front. Doors and drawer fronts cover the box (full overlay) with an even gap all round. What a 600 mm base cabinet (870 high, 560 deep) is made of with the millimetre rules: Part Size (mm) Edge banding Side × 2 720 × 560 × 18 front edge Bottom 564 × 560 × 18 front edge Front rail 564 × 100 × 18 front edge Back rail 564 × 100 × 18 none Back 580 × 710 × 8 none Shelf 562 × 516 × 18 front edge Door 717 × 597 × 18 all four edges Plinth 600 × 150 × 18 none The box is 870 − 150 = 720 high. Inside, it is 600 − 2 × 18 = 564 wide. The back goes 8 mm into each groove. The shelf is 1 mm short at each end and stops 20 mm behind the front. The door is 3 mm smaller than the box each way, so two doors side by side have a 3 mm gap. Sizes are finished sizes, with the banding on. The cut list also gives the cut size: the banding's thickness comes off each banded edge. Doors and drawers A cabinet wider than one door may be (600 mm or 24 in) gets a pair. A tall cabinet whose door would be taller than 1400 mm (55 in) gets two rows of doors with a fixed shelf between them. Properties › Doors sets one, two or none. A drawer base has fronts of equal height (or the heights you give). Each drawer box is as wide as the opening less the slide gap at each side (13 mm), as deep as the longest slide that fits, and 40 mm lower than its front. Its bottom sits in 6 mm grooves. Changing the rules Settings › CadCraft › Cabinets sets the carcass thickness, the gap between fronts, the back (in grooves, between the panels, or on the back), the back's thickness and the toe kick (legs and a plinth, notched sides, or none). The settings apply to cabinets you place from then on: each cabinet keeps the rules it was built with, so a drawing gives the same cut list wherever it is opened. A drafting aid. The rules are common shop practice, not any maker's system. Check the sizes against the hardware you will fit (hinges, slides, legs) before you cut."},{"slug":"cabinet-not-built-yet","scope":"addon:cabinet","title":"Cabinets: not built yet","summary":"What the Cabinets add-on does not do yet, so you know before you plan a job around it.","section":"reference","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","missing","roadmap","cabinets","g-code","drilling","face frame"],"headings":[{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EG-code straight from CadCraft.\u003C/strong\u003E The nested sheets go out as a DXF; PartCraft turns that DXF into G-code through its post processors (a program for each sheet) once its Manufacture entry for nested sheets is mounted. Until then your CAM program reads the DXF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrilling files for a drilling machine.\u003C/strong\u003E The holes are listed and exported as a CSV; no machine format (boring-machine files) is written yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFace-frame cabinets.\u003C/strong\u003E Only frameless boxes are built for now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECorner cabinets, end panels, fillers and worktops\u003C/strong\u003E as parts of their own. A gap (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EG\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECABINET\u003C/code\u003E) leaves the space.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EQR codes\u003C/strong\u003E on the labels (a Code 128 bar code is there).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBar optimisation for profiles\u003C/strong\u003E (lengths cut from bars): that comes with the window builder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet G-code straight from CadCraft. The nested sheets go out as a DXF; PartCraft turns that DXF into G-code through its post processors (a program for each sheet) once its Manufacture entry for nested sheets is mounted. Until then your CAM program reads the DXF. Drilling files for a drilling machine. The holes are listed and exported as a CSV; no machine format (boring-machine files) is written yet. Face-frame cabinets. Only frameless boxes are built for now. Corner cabinets, end panels, fillers and worktops as parts of their own. A gap (G in CABINET) leaves the space. QR codes on the labels (a Code 128 bar code is there). Bar optimisation for profiles (lengths cut from bars): that comes with the window builder."}]};
